>
>
> > If I were writing a program to parse lojban, how should my parser deal
> with
> > a piece of discourse that contained {go'i} if it wanted to expand
> > everything?
> > i.e. given the following bridi
> > .i ti'e .u'i la .bob. noi lazni cu zvati to pe'i cinri toi ma
> > How does {go'i} differ syntactically and semantically from {zo'e zvati
> zo'e}
>
> Neither seems like a very helpful answer to a "ma" question.
>
> mu'o mi'e xorxes


I know, I was just throwing as much interesting stuff into a predicate as I
could think of at the time and then saying "go'i" as a way of asking "do
{ma} get repeated in a go'i?"

Based on the rest of your email, it sounds like a good rule of thumb is
"read {go'i} like it's repeating only the main selbri of the previous bridi
where it's places are filled with {zo'e} which usually means to assume the
previous bridi's terbri(s)".

